      "rationale": "If pendulum_lab and pendulum_obs are reading the same physical pendulum but showing near-zero correlation (−0.323, within chance), a constant bias in one channel would explain why they do not track: they are offset by a fixed amount. Similarly, resonator_lab and resonator_obs show weak correlation (0.348). A fixed conversion error, clock miscalibration, or systematic offset in the data acquisition path of one instrument would preserve baseline stability and lack of drift while destroying cross-instrument agreement. Today's data show all four streams continuing without obvious artifact, but this does not rule out constant bias in any of them.",
      "evidenceFor": [
        "pendulum_lab and pendulum_obs should be highly correlated if measuring the same system; instead, r = −0.323 (chance ±0.374)",
        "resonator_lab and resonator_obs should be correlated; instead, r = 0.348 (weak, near chance ±0.529)",
        "Value spacings and lack of quantization artifacts rule out obvious digital truncation, but not a constant gain or offset error"
      ],
      "evidenceAgainst": [
        "Internal consistency within each instrument (no sudden jumps, reasonable value distributions) argues against complete failure",
        "Longest exact repetition runs are at chance level (1 of N trials), ruling out data logger replay or stuck values"
      ]
    },
    {
      "label": "Physical coupling or environmental modulation exists but operates on timescales longer than 10 days or with very small amplitude; baseline period was too short to establish it",
