  "summaryOfChange": "QUESTION REPLACEMENT: Prior working hypotheses addressed baseline stability and drift detection in isolation. Today's review reveals the core anomaly is NOT drift but LACK OF COHERENCE: four measurement streams with reasonable baselines and no detected drift nonetheless show near-zero or negative cross-correlations and weak autocorrelations, all within or near chance levels. This is the unexplained pattern. Hypothesis set rebuilt from first principles around this observation. PRIMARY: noise-dominated measurement (0.45) — high measurement error relative to signal is mundane and explains all observations. SECONDARY: baseline offset or calibration issue (0.25) and systematic readout bias (0.15) — instruments stable but decoupled. TERTIARY: long-timescale environmental variation (0.10) and data labeling error (0.05). No hypothesis above 0.5 reflects genuine uncertainty: the data are consistent with multiple benign explanations, none of which are exotic or require major equipment failure. CRITICAL IMPLICATION: continuation of the baseline campaign will not resolve this anomaly. A post-baseline period alone will not tell us whether instruments are noisy or offset. NEXT ACTION (not a hypothesis): I must perform direct cross-checks—measure the same standard reference object (pendulum or resonator) with two instruments simultaneously, or recalibrate instruments against each other using a known standard. Baseline period should be extended under controlled conditions, or independent verification sought from colleagues with separate apparatus."
}
